 "text": "The forger wants something harder: different bytes that really do have the right name. Remember the three games from episode three? The forger is playing one of the last two, and which one depends on a question that's easy to skip past. Who made the original?\n\nSuppose the original was made by someone else. It's your file, or a document published by somebody honest. Then the forger has to find a second preimage, different content with the same C-I-D as content they had no part in creating. With SHA five-twelve, the generic cost of that is around two to the five hundred twelve tries. That's not happening.\n\nBut now suppose the forger made the original. Then they get to play the easier game. They don't need to hit your target. They just need any two documents of their own design with matching names. One harmless, one harmful. That's a collision, and thanks to the birthday effect, the generic cost drops to about two to the two hundred fifty-six.\n\nHere's why that matters. Picture it as a story. The forger sends you a contract. You read it carefully, you approve it, and you record its C-I-D. Later, you fetch that C-I-D, from somewhere the forger has influence, and you get the other twin. You verify it. And the check passes. Because, as far as two fifty-six tee is concerned, both twins genuinely have that name.\n\nSo verification defeats the liar completely, but it can't defeat a true collision. That's the honest crack in \"a C-I-D uniquely determines its content.\" The question is how wide the crack is.",
